{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WYQyxcojgyBHfYkApZpoqk","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now I have enough information to provide a comprehensive analysis. Let me compile the findings:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report: Almanac Codebase\n\n**Top 5-8 Friction Points (severity-ordered):**\n\n1. **Converge control-file wrapper duplicates harden pattern without abstraction** \n   - `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh:59` (`almanac_converge_control_file`) and `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh:1614` (`almanac_harden_control_file`)\n   - Both follow identical shape: base + kind → adapter → signal-file basename, with converge explicitly noting \"matching almanac_harden_control_file\" (line 58 comment). The wrapper is trivial but lives in two places; adding a third loop means a third copy unless refactored to a shared helper in `lib/run.sh`.\n   - **Why it's friction:** Locality scattered; a change to the contract (e.g., adding a new signal kind) requires updates across multiple -core files. Testability fragmented across test-harden.sh / test-converge.sh.\n\n2. **Converge auto-commit logic is embedded, not behind a clean interface**\n   - `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh:679-712` (`almanac_converge_commit_agent_paths`). Auto-commit is converge-specific (dirty-before/after snapshotting, staged-path filtering), baked into the prompt-mode worker round. No abstraction for a future loop that might want similar behavior.\n   - **Why it's friction:** Understanding requires bouncing between the snapshot setup (line 754), the worker call (line 763), and the commit cleanup (line 711). If ralph or a new loop needs intelligent commit logic, there's no seam to plug into — converge owns the full stack. Deletion test: removing converge leaves duplicate git machinery scattered.\n\n3. **Converge and harden each own role-wrapper functions instead of reusing shared resolver**\n   - `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh:89-104` (`almanac_converge_role` / `_field`) vs. `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh` (uses `almanac_loop_role_config` directly). Converge wraps the shared resolver with a loop-specific interface; harden calls the shared one directly.\n   - **Why it's friction:** Two call patterns for the same concern. Shallow wrapper that adds no value but creates a knowledge tax — callers must know whether to call the loop-specific or shared version. No leverage: deleting the wrapper would simplify both.\n\n4. **Argparse patterns in cmd/converge.sh and cmd/harden.sh diverge unnecessarily**\n   - `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/cmd/converge.sh:30-102` vs. `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/cmd/harden.sh:47-96`. Converge uses `{ usage >&2; _die \"...\" }` inline; harden uses bare `[ \"$#\" -gt 0 ] || _die ...` and a single `usage` call at the top. Neither is wrong, but divergence means each new loop/command ships its own variant. Ralph delegates entirely to the launcher, so it has no argparse (good).\n   - **Why it's friction:** Cognitive overhead reading multiple CLI surfaces. Inconsistent error UX. No shared argparse skeleton for future commands.\n\n5. **Converge lacks a loop adapter — it's an on-brand adhoc CLI, not a loop under the seam**\n   - `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/loops/converge.sh` exists and declares control (signal files), but launch (`exec_argv`) yields `bash $ALMANAC_HOME/bin/almanac converge --goal …` — a bare CLI invocation, not a runner script like ralph or harden. Converge is launched through `cmd/converge.sh`'s main body, not the launcher (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`).\n   - **Why it's friction:** Converge is a hybrid — it uses the run registry and signal-file seam but bypasses the launcher and doesn't expose config prompting through the hub's New-run flow the way harden does. Adding interactive config (e.g., choosing agent vs overseer provider) would require retrofitting the launcher, not extending converge naturally.\n\n6. **Converge overseer verdict parsing is fragile — falls back to CONTINUE on malformed output**\n   - `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh:267-334`. Parser is permissive (missing fields → returns 0 cleanly, verdict defaults to CONTINUE). No validation surface; if overseer emits incomplete output, the loop silently treats it as \"keep going\" instead of surfacing the error.\n   - **Why it's friction:** Hard to diagnose convergence loop stalls. Parser logic is novel (not reused from harden's ratify verdict, which is also conservative but separate). Two-loop system means fragmented verdict validation that should be unified.\n\n7. **Converge runs are registered in the shared registry but status rendering is custom**\n   - `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh:385-430` (`almanac_converge_status`/watch/stop). Ralph and harden use the shared hub's `almanac_loop_read_run` / `almanac_loop_update_run_progress`; converge writes its own status rendering. Both live in -core.sh, so the loop logic is entangled with CLI concern.\n   - **Why it's friction:** Duplication across loop types. Converge's status queries are split between lib/run.sh (the registry) and converge-core.sh (the rendering). Understanding the full status lifecycle requires reading two modules in different contexts.\n\n8. **Test duplication: converge verdict handling scattered without shared validation scaffold**\n   - `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/tests/test-converge.sh` has verdict-parse tests (lines 450+), overseer-stops-loop (line 802), etc. Harden's tests live in test-harden.sh with a different structure. No shared `almanac_validate_verdict` or test helper — each loop implements its own, so adding a third loop means a third test scaffold.\n   - **Why it's friction:** Validation logic is scattered; a bug in one loop's verdict parsing isn't caught by the other's tests. Deletion test: test-converge.sh has no extraction point — converge-specific concerns and shared concerns are interleaved.\n\n**Key insight:** Converge is **functionally complete and correct**, but it's a *sibling loop*, not a fully fledged adapter citizen. It shares the registry and signal-file seam but owns its own argparse, status rendering, verdict validation, and role wrapper. The tension is that the engine was designed for ralph/harden (provider + lens fan-out patterns) and converge's simpler model (one agent + one overseer per round) doesn't leverage existing abstractions — it reinvents them locally instead of extending the seam. Ralph solution: **extract shared control-file helper**, **unify verdict validation** (move parse + fallback-conservative logic to lib/run.sh as a reusable predicate), and **route converge through the launcher** so interactive config prompting works end-to-end."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14593634-940e-4f64-9481-34cf5bee965a","uuid":"0cca3267-a8b6-4afb-a8ae-0ab4568338e3","timestamp":"2026-05-26T13:47:57.327Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Walk the almanac codebase at /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac and surface architectural friction. Context: this is a bash CLI/skills toolkit. The domain model is documented in CONTEXT.md (terms: Loop, Runner, Provider, Adapter, Run, Launcher, Hub, Overseer, Module, Interface, Implementation, Depth, Seam). Map is in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md and lib/ files.\n\nGoal: find shallow modules, leaky seams, parallel branching on provider/loop names outside adapters, duplication across cmd/* scripts, places where understanding requires bouncing between files.\n\nFor each finding, report:\n- File path(s) + relevant function/section\n- One-sentence problem\n- Why it's friction (locality lost? leverage thin? deletion test passes?)\n\nSpecifically check:\n1. lib/converge-core.sh — newly added converge loop. Is it a loop adapter or its own thing? Does it duplicate harden-core.sh patterns?\n2. lib/loops/ adapters — does converge live there?\n3. cmd/converge.sh — argparse: does it follow patterns from cmd/ralph.sh + cmd/harden.sh? Or fork?\n4. lib/agent.sh `agent_capture`/`agent_stream`/`agent_raw` — is converge using them or rolling its own?\n5. Provider/loop discovery — are there any hardcoded branches on `if [[ $loop == \"converge\" ]]` outside of adapters?\n6. cmd/* scripts — any using raw `echo` for errors instead of `_die`/`_info` (per CLAUDE.md project rules)?\n7. Test files — duplication, scattered validation logic that should live in `almanac_validate_skill`?\n8. Auto-commit logic in converge — does it live in worker run code, or behind a clean interface?\n9. Symlink resolution / ALMANAC_HOME bootstrap — consistent across entry points?\n10. Any \"barrel\" sourcing where a script sources more lib/ than it actually uses?\n\nReport top 5–8 findings ordered by friction severity. Under 600 words.","agentId":"a157451fd3c81afe7","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now I have enough information to provide a comprehensive analysis. Let me compile the findings:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report: Almanac Codebase\n\n**Top 5-8 Friction Points (severity-ordered):**\n\n1. **Converge control-file wrapper duplicates harden pattern without abstraction** \n   - `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh:59` (`almanac_converge_control_file`) and `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh:1614` (`almanac_harden_control_file`)\n   - Both follow identical shape: base + kind → adapter → signal-file basename, with converge explicitly noting \"matching almanac_harden_control_file\" (line 58 comment). The wrapper is trivial but lives in two places; adding a third loop means a third copy unless refactored to a shared h